WP48 stage 1. A second runtime build, compiled with -DNISH_ARENA_RANGE, puts every arena chunk of 2 MiB or less on one reserved address range. scripts/build.sh takes --arena-range for it and src/run-cache.ts keys it. The compiler selects it only for a program with a compact class, and none exists until stage 2, so no program's IR and no byte of the default runtime change.

On Linux the range is fixed at [1 GiB, 16 GiB) and reserved with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE. A busy address panics before main, and a refused mapping has its own panic. On macOS the range goes anywhere, with its base in nish_ref_base. The first 64 bytes are never handed out. Chunks are carved in power-of-two classes from 4 KiB to 2 MiB, with a locked free list per class; exact chunks over 2 MiB stay on malloc. Exhausting the range panics and names the 15 GiB limit.

Every C bump and relocation site rounds the arena offset up to 8 in this build. That covers the struct and text bumps, nish_cross_in, keep and region adopt, so stage 2's 4-byte compact instances cannot misalign the rest. In-place array growth never reallocs a range chunk: it copies out instead. --debug-arena takes its never-reused reservation from the range, and the ASan build reserves nothing.

The design note's §7 records the owner's decisions of 2026-11-17:
- accepted;
- the compiler picks compact classes, with implements Compact and implements Wide to force or refuse;
- the zero-based decode on Linux and Windows, based on macOS;
- fields only;
- 4-byte units;
- a busy fixed address panics.

The golden runner now splits text into lines with splitLinesLanes, a lane-wise split in tests/nish/lines.ts. It writes a newline flag per position, compares sixteen flags at a time with nish:simd (eq, bitmask) and walks the set bits. A scalar tail handles the last few positions. It is used everywhere tests/nish/run.ts split lines, and gives the same lines as std/text splitLines at every length.

std/text.ts is unchanged. The compiler records every nish:simd load as an index site it cannot prove, so a lane-wise splitLines in std/text would make every --deny-panics caller of splitLines fail to build. The runner is not built under --deny-panics. The other candidate loops stay scalar, as profiled:
- the stdout diff runs only when a test fails;
- the line compare in std/testing is 1.6% of the run and already uses glibc's memcmp;
- stripHeader's scanning is the line split itself.

Three new golden programs each compute one answer two ways, a scalar loop and a sixteen-lane nish:simd loop with a scalar tail, and assert that the two agree:
- simd_byte_runs: first index, count and run length;
- simd_adler32: Adler-32, also held to the published value of "Wikipedia";
- simd_hex: lower-case hex.

Lengths 0, 1, 15, 16, 17, 31, 32, 33 and a 100-byte tail cover the empty input, the tail alone, one block and blocks plus a tail. The scalar twins elsewhere in the suite are unchanged.

tests/link/std_text_split_lines_simd holds splitLinesLanes to std/text splitLines, natively and under Node, across:
- lengths 0 to 48, 4095, 4096, 4097 and 8193;
- a newline at each chunk edge;
- \r\n endings, U+010A and a surrogate pair.

Measured: runner instructions on the simd_ filter, --jobs 1, cachegrind: 30,714,650 (main) and 30,711,365 (main, second build; band 0.01%) to 26,515,940, -13.7%
Tests: tests/cases/simd_byte_runs
Tests: tests/cases/simd_adler32
Tests: tests/cases/simd_hex
Tests: tests/link/std_text_split_lines_simd
Part of #1706

A pull request that changes nothing a side job reads no longer pays for that job. A new `scope` job runs scripts/ci-scope.mjs over the pull request's changed files and labels, and writes one `<lane>=true|false` output for each of seven side jobs: bootstrap, nish-cmp, nish-cmp elision, the NEON path, SHA-256 on Apple silicon, fuzz-smoke and postgres-live. When a lane is off, its job skips every work step, says why in its summary, moves to an ubuntu-latest runner, and still reports success under its usual name. The `test` shards and `lint` are never lanes and never wait on `scope`.

The rule fails closed. A lane is off only when every changed path is on that lane's allowlist of paths that none of its commands read; every lane whose job runs `node tests/run.js` reads all of tests/. Every lane runs in each of these cases:
- any event other than a pull request, which includes pushes to main and the release;
- the `ci:full` label (add it, then re-run all jobs);
- `NISH_CI_SCOPE=full`;
- a changed-file list that is unreadable, empty or truncated;
- a deleted file;
- a rename that crosses a read set;
- a change to .github/, a package file or the scope script.

`ci-gate` needs every job, runs always, and fails unless each job succeeded or was skipped by its own `if:`. It can become the one required check.

tests/run.js pins, per lane, a file that each of its commands reads. The `test` job delegates the fixed point to bootstrap and the sanitizers to fuzz-smoke, and it holds those delegations only while bootstrap's and fuzz-smoke's lanes read the delegated checks' inputs. The runtime_sanitize step is held to pipefail, a `passed, 0 failed` line and no SKIP line, with no `|| true` or continue-on-error.

A replay of 60 merged pull requests found no failing job that the scope would have skipped. Their recorded failures cover only nish-cmp, fuzz-smoke and the always-run jobs, so the replay is no evidence for the other five lanes. The one excused failure, #1666's fuzz-smoke toolchain download, is pinned by name. A docs-only probe (#1747) ran this workflow with every lane off: every check concluded, the lane jobs took 2–7 s each, and the run used about 66 runner-minutes.

Measured: the backtest skips 17.5 of the lanes' 41.6 runner-minutes per pull request run, 14.7% of a ~119-minute run, with 0 misses over 60 merged PRs. Docs-only probe run 44280413840: 65.8 runner-minutes, all lanes off.
Refs: docs/wp10-ci.md#scoped-lanes, #1734
Tests: node tests/run.js ci-scope, node tests/run.js ci.yml, node scripts/ci-scope.mjs --backtest
Fixes #1724
